WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.357 --> 00:00:02.006
(sea lions barking)

00:00:02.006 --> 00:00:05.977
- I kind of compare it with
being at a giant dog park.

00:00:05.977 --> 00:00:07.010
Some of them love it.

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:08.821
Like you start giving
them arm pit scratches

00:00:08.821 --> 00:00:10.713
and they're just like giant dogs.

00:00:10.713 --> 00:00:12.107
They just start rolling around,

00:00:12.107 --> 00:00:13.477
and they're flying all around you.

00:00:13.477 --> 00:00:15.404
But these animals are huge.

00:00:15.404 --> 00:00:16.600
People are intimidated by them,

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.713
but once you, once you
learn their behavior,

00:00:18.713 --> 00:00:20.155
they're just right into you right away.

00:00:20.155 --> 00:00:22.515
They just look at you like a big chew toy.

00:00:22.515 --> 00:00:25.682
(soft peaceful music)

00:00:27.175 --> 00:00:28.212
(motor running)

00:00:28.212 --> 00:00:30.626
(uptempo piano music)

00:00:30.626 --> 00:00:32.733
I could never see myself living anywhere

00:00:32.733 --> 00:00:34.241
other than the Pacific Northwest.

00:00:34.241 --> 00:00:35.171
(wind blowing)

00:00:35.171 --> 00:00:36.983
I could literally spend every single day

00:00:36.983 --> 00:00:38.086
of the rest of my life

00:00:38.086 --> 00:00:39.688
and never travel anywhere

00:00:39.688 --> 00:00:40.521
other than Vancouver Island

00:00:40.521 --> 00:00:41.627
and it would still never have enough time

00:00:41.627 --> 00:00:42.585
to see this whole place.

00:00:42.585 --> 00:00:43.894
(water splashes)

00:00:43.894 --> 00:00:45.845
My first introduction
to the ocean would have

00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:47.819
definitely come from my mom.

00:00:47.819 --> 00:00:49.746
That's me with a sea lion right there.

00:00:49.746 --> 00:00:50.605
Later on in life,

00:00:50.605 --> 00:00:52.498
I realized it is pretty unique experience

00:00:52.498 --> 00:00:54.321
to get to help and spend
time with these animals

00:00:54.321 --> 00:00:55.868
on a regular basis.

00:00:55.868 --> 00:00:57.119
(sea lions barking)

00:00:57.119 --> 00:01:00.335
Sea lions are a large intelligent animal

00:01:00.335 --> 00:01:03.911
and they're very sensitive
of what your intentions are

00:01:03.911 --> 00:01:06.128
and how they choose to interact with you

00:01:06.128 --> 00:01:07.788
is totally their choice.

00:01:07.788 --> 00:01:10.087
If you're comfortable
and their inquisitive

00:01:10.087 --> 00:01:11.863
they'll come and they'll chew on you fins,

00:01:11.863 --> 00:01:14.290
or come up and they chew
on the edge of your mask,

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:15.973
or give you, uh, big kisses on the lips,

00:01:15.973 --> 00:01:17.169
or big hugs.

00:01:17.169 --> 00:01:21.336
They wrap their fins around
you and give you a big hug.

00:01:23.348 --> 00:01:25.014
People get sketched out about it.

00:01:25.014 --> 00:01:26.631
(soft relaxing music)

00:01:26.631 --> 00:01:28.156
Like I've had lots of people
contact me and be like,

00:01:28.156 --> 00:01:30.370
"Don't you think those
things are aggressive?"

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:32.239
Just because an animal's
putting it's mouth

00:01:32.239 --> 00:01:35.037
on you doesn't mean it's
doing it in a harmful way.

00:01:35.037 --> 00:01:36.059
They don't have hands,

00:01:36.059 --> 00:01:37.823
they can't explore their world

00:01:37.823 --> 00:01:40.906
with anything other than their mouth.

00:01:41.945 --> 00:01:44.081
There's people that
spend, now, a lot of money

00:01:44.081 --> 00:01:45.567
to travel to the other side of the planet

00:01:45.567 --> 00:01:47.622
to watch a group of
large migratory animals

00:01:47.622 --> 00:01:49.491
in Africa or something, say,

00:01:49.491 --> 00:01:51.465
and here I can just go out on my boat

00:01:51.465 --> 00:01:54.147
and it's not something I take
for granted at all anymore,

00:01:54.147 --> 00:01:56.980
having the opportunity to do this.

00:02:00.605 --> 00:02:03.355
(relaxing music)

